Suppose that $X$ and $Y$ are ordered linear spaces.  A map $T : X_+ \to Y$ is called \emph {superadditive}
if $T (x + y) \geqslant T (x) + T (y)$ for all $x, y \in X_+$; $T$ is called \emph {additive} if
$T (x + y) = T (x) + T (y)$ for all $x, y \in X_+$.

An ordered linear space $X$ is said to satisfy the \emph {Riesz Decomposition Property} if $[0, x] + [0, y] = [0, x + y]$
for all $x, y \in X_+$;
here and elsewhere a sum of sets $A$ and $B$ in a linear space is the Minkowski sum $A + B = \{a + b \mid a \in A, \,\, b \in B\}$.

By the bounded aperture property assumption there exists some $c \in \mathbb R$ such that
\begin{equation} B \subset \{s (x_0 - y_0) + y \mid |s| \leqslant c, \,\, y \in H \}.
\end{equation}

Let $x_0 \in [0, x] + [0, y]$.
Using Proposition~\ref {aocsd}, we may assume that
\begin{equation} z \in A = \mathrm{int} \left( [0, x + y] \setminus \left([0, x] + [0, y]\right) \right) \subset [0, x + y] \cap \{z \mid g (z) > \alpha\}
\end{equation}
with some linear functional $g$.

Indeed, suppose that under the assumptions of Proposition~\ref {rktinf}
map $S : X_+ \to Y$ is superlinear and $S \geqslant T_j$ for all $1 \leqslant j \leqslant n$.
Then for any $x \in X_+$ and $x_j \in X_+$, $1 \leqslant j \leqslant n$, such that $\sum_{j = 1}^n x_j \leqslant x$, we have
\begin{equation} S (x) \geqslant \sum_{j = 1}^n S (x_j) \geqslant \sum_{j = 1}^n T_j (x_j)
\end{equation}
by superadditivity of $S$, and \eqref {sssup} implies $S \geqslant \rkts {T}$.

It follows that 
\begin{multline*}
\rkt {T} {x} \leqslant
\sup \left\{ \sum_{j = 1}^n T_j (x_j) \mid x_j \in X_+ \text { for $1 \leqslant j \leqslant n$ and } \sum_{j = 1}^n x_j = x \right\},
\end{multline*}
which establishes \eqref {rktdefeq}.

By changing the sign of $T$ if necessary we may assume that
\begin{equation} T z > \sup \{T (u + v) \mid u \in [0, x], v \in [0, y]\}.
\end{equation}

Taking supremums of the right-hand part of \eqref {tztuv2} one after the other yields
\begin{multline*}
T z \geqslant y_0 + \sup \{T u \mid u \in [0, x]\} +
\sup \{T v \mid v \in [0, y]\} =
\\
y_0 + \rktp {T} {x} + \rktp {T} {y},
\end{multline*}
which implies that
\begin{equation} \rktp {T} {x + y} \geqslant T z \geqslant y_0 + \rktp {T} {x} + \rktp {T} {y}.
\end{equation}

Indeed, suppose that $\rkt {T} {x} = y$ for some $x \in X_+$ and $y \in \mathbb R$ and we are given an open neighbourhood
$(y - \varepsilon, y + \varepsilon) \subset U \subset \mathbb R$ of $y$, $\varepsilon > 0$;
we need to find an open neighbourhood $V \subset X_+$ of $x$ satisfying $\rkt {T} {V} \subset U$.

In order to prove Proposition~\ref {suprtrdpf} we modify this proof as follows.
Surely we need to take any $x, y \in X_+$ and not just $x, y \in \mathrm{int} X_+$.  As in the proof of Theorem~\ref {suprtf}
we equip $X$ with its order topology.
Then Lemma~\ref {suprtrdpf} instead of Lemma~\ref {suprtli} gives $S_0$ and $S_1$ satisfying inequalities $<$ in \eqref {s0s1}
rather than equalities, and thus we get inequality $>$ instead of the first equality in \eqref {sestrk}.
The rest of the proof does not change.

